# itel labels 26 vendor HIDL interfaces that device/mediatek/sepolicy_vndr does
# not. Most are for Transsion services this tree deliberately does not ship, and
# a label for an interface nothing serves is dead weight — so only the ones with
# a shipped provider AND a measured denial are carried here.
#
# The Goodix daemon is the one that qualifies: the fingerprint HAL we ship tries
# to publish it at start and is refused. All four spellings share one type in
# stock; only IGoodixFingerprintDaemon was observed being added on build 61, and
# the siblings are carried with it because they are the same interface family
# and would fail the same way.
#
# Deliberately NOT carried, with reasons, so this is not re-litigated:
#   ITrancamserver          ✅ NOW CARRIED (build 78). This entry used to defer it:
#                           "the provider IS shipped but never starts — its binary
#                           has no exec type ... that needs a domain of its own;
#                           separate change." That change is sepolicy/vendor/
#                           trancamserver.te, so the objection no longer holds —
#                           the interface is no longer labelled for something
#                           nothing serves. Verified on build 77 hardware first:
#                           the binary's whole closure ships, it runs, and it
#                           registers ITrancamserver/default (lshal server-pid Y).
#   IFpsensorHidlSvc        STILL NOT CARRIED, and now with the measurement behind
#                           it. This unit's sensor is GOODIX (/dev/goodix_fp);
#                           IFpsensorHidlSvc is declared only by stock's
#                           manifest_chipone.xml — a DIFFERENT sensor vendor's
#                           fragment, arriving wholesale via DEVICE_MANIFEST_FILE —
#                           and NO binary or library anywhere in the rev 28 vendor
#                           partition references fpsensorhidlsvc. There is no
#                           server to unblock, so a label would be dead weight.
#                           Silenced instead, in sepolicy/vendor/dontaudit.te,
#                           which is what this entry said the honest instrument
#                           would be.
#   INxpNfc, INxpNfcLegacy  ✅ NOW CARRIED (build 78) — and this REVERSES the
#                           decision this entry recorded twice. The 2026-08-24
#                           correction was right that the finds fire every boot
#                           from a HEALTHY HAL, and wrong about the reason it drew
#                           from that: it concluded "NOTHING in stock or here
#                           serves them, so labelling them would point the HAL at a
#                           service that does not exist." Nobody had checked the
#                           binary. Build 77, our own shipped NFC HAL:
#                             vendor::nxp::nxpnfc::V2_0::INxpNfc::registerAsService
#                             vendor::nxp::nxpnfclegacy::V1_0::INxpNfcLegacy::
#                                                              registerAsService
#                           It carries the SERVER side of both, with the method
#                           bodies (resetEse, setNxpTransitConfig, NciTransceive,
#                           getEseState ...). It was never a client looking up
#                           something absent — it is a server that was never
#                           allowed to publish, exactly like pnpmgr. Full reasoning,
#                           and what is still unproven, in hal_nfc_default.te.
#                           🔑 The lesson: "nothing serves it" was inferred from
#                           the MANIFEST both times. The provider is a property of
#                           the BINARY, and one `strings` would have settled it in
#                           either direction.
vendor.goodix.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint::IGoodixFingerprintDaemon          u:object_r:goodix_fingerprint_service:s0
vendor.goodix.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint::IGoodixFingerprintDaemonExt       u:object_r:goodix_fingerprint_service:s0
vendor.goodix.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint::IGoodixFingerprintDaemonHbd       u:object_r:goodix_fingerprint_service:s0
vendor.goodix.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint::IGoodixFingerprintDaemonFido      u:object_r:goodix_fingerprint_service:s0
vendor.transsion.hardware.trancam.trancamserver::ITrancamserver                   u:object_r:hal_trancamserver_hwservice:s0
vendor.nxp.nxpnfc::INxpNfc                                                      u:object_r:nxpnfc_hwservice:s0
vendor.nxp.nxpnfclegacy::INxpNfcLegacy                                          u:object_r:nxpnfc_hwservice:s0

# 🔴 Wi-Fi HOTSPOT. Stock's vendor_hwservice_contexts:91, byte for byte.
#
# Without this line the SoftAP is DEAD — not degraded, dead — and no gate this
# project owns could see it, because hostapd is a lazy HAL that only starts when
# a hotspot is requested. Measured on v2 build 1, clean install:
#
#   framework asks AIDL IHostapd -> absent -> falls back to HIDL @1.0
#   hwservicemanager starts /vendor/bin/hw/hostapd as a lazy HAL
#   avc: denied { find } for interface=vendor.transsion.hardware.wifi.hostapd
#        ::IHostapd  tcontext=u:object_r:default_android_hwservice:s0
#   HidlServiceManagement: ... must be in VINTF manifest in order to register/get
#   hostapd: Failed to initialize HIDL interface
#   F libc: Fatal signal 11 (SIGSEGV) in hostapd_hidl_deinit   <- its own cleanup
#   WifiNative: Failed to start hostapd -> SoftApManager -> SAP failed to start
#
# Everything else was already right, checked one at a time rather than assumed:
#   configs/vintf/manifest/android.hardware.wifi.hostapd.xml is BYTE-IDENTICAL
#     to stock's and declares both this interface and android.hardware.wifi
#     .hostapd@1.3, so the manifest half needs no change
#   hal_wifi_hostapd_hwservice is an AOSP type (system/sepolicy/public/
#     hwservice.te:55), so nothing new is declared here
#   hal_attribute_hwservice(hal_wifi_hostapd, hal_wifi_hostapd_hwservice) in
#     system/sepolicy/public/hal_wifi_hostapd.te:5 already grants ADD to the
#     attribute hal_wifi_hostapd_default carries
#   /vendor/bin/hw/hostapd ships and carries the Transsion V1_0 server symbols
# The ONLY divergence from stock was this label. Same shape as the NXP pair above.
#
# ⚠ THIS CLASS CANNOT BE LIVE-TESTED ON THIS DEVICE, and two instruments were
# spent proving that rather than the fix. hwservicemanager reads the
# *_hwservice_contexts files before post-fs-data, so a meta-overlayfs module
# carrying a patched file takes on /vendor and changes NOTHING (verified: the
# line was present on /vendor after reboot, Enforcing, and the denial was
# byte-identical). `ksud sepolicy patch` then reported rc=0 and the denial still
# fired 3x — VOID, not negative, exactly as the 2026-08-25 NFC attempt.
# `ksud sepolicy check` validates SYNTAX only: a check that cannot fail.
# file_contexts, property_contexts and hwservice_contexts are all read before
# the overlay mounts. They must be proven in a BUILD.
vendor.transsion.hardware.wifi.hostapd::IHostapd                                u:object_r:hal_wifi_hostapd_hwservice:s0
